Upgrade to PHP 8.4
******************

.[perex]
When you upgrade to PHP 8.4, DressCode turns a `foreach` that looks for the first match into `array_find()`, `int $x = null` into `?int $x = null`, and `(new Foo())->bar()` into `new Foo()->bar()`. It fixes the PHP 8.4 deprecations that have a direct replacement and reports the rest, such as `E_STRICT`. In the same run, it dresses the rewritten code in your coding standard.


What It Looks Like
==================

The class `Invoice` is written for PHP 8.3. Once the project requires PHP 8.4, six places in it change. The configuration adds the sets `modernizations` and `deprecations` to the standard. `nameResolution: certain` says that the namespaces of the project declare no functions of their own, so `round()` and `fputcsv()` are certainly the built-in functions of PHP. The key `targets` gives the target PHP version, which DressCode would otherwise read from `composer.json`:

```neon
use:
	- perCs
	- modernizations
	- deprecations

nameResolution: certain

targets:
	php: '8.4'
```

```php .[before]
final class Invoice
{
    /** @param list<Item> $items */
    public function __construct(
        private array $items,
    ) {}

    public function hasGift(): bool
    {
        foreach ($this->items as $item) {
            if ($item->isGift()) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    public function findItem(string $sku): ?Item
    {
        foreach ($this->items as $item) {
            if ($item->sku === $sku) {
                return $item;
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    public function price(Item $item, Discount $discount = null): float
    {
        return round($item->price * ($discount?->ratio ?? 1), 2, PHP_ROUND_HALF_UP);
    }

    public function export(string $file): void
    {
        $handle = fopen($file, 'w');
        foreach ($this->items as $item) {
            fputcsv($handle, [$item->sku, $this->price($item)]);
        }
    }

    public function render(): string
    {
        return (new InvoiceTemplate())->render($this->items);
    }
}
```

After one `dresscode fix`:

```php .[after]
final class Invoice
{
    /** @param list<Item> $items */
    public function __construct(
        private array $items,
    ) {}

    public function hasGift(): bool
    {
        return array_any($this->items, fn($item) => $item->isGift());
    }

    public function findItem(string $sku): ?Item
    {
        return array_find($this->items, fn($item) => $item->sku === $sku);
    }

    public function price(Item $item, ?Discount $discount = null): float
    {
        return round($item->price * ($discount?->ratio ?? 1), 2, RoundingMode::HalfAwayFromZero);
    }

    public function export(string $file): void
    {
        $handle = fopen($file, 'w');
        foreach ($this->items as $item) {
            fputcsv($handle, [$item->sku, $this->price($item)], escape: '\\');
        }
    }

    public function render(): string
    {
        return new InvoiceTemplate()->render($this->items);
    }
}
```

Four of the changes use what PHP 8.4 added: the two loops became `array_any()` and `array_find()`, the rounding mode is now a case of the enum `RoundingMode`, and `new` no longer needs parentheses around it. The other two deal with what PHP 8.4 deprecated: the parameter `$discount` got its `?`, and `fputcsv()` passes its `escape` argument explicitly, with the same backslash it used by default, so the CSV file stays the same.


What Gets Rewritten
===================

The set `modernizations` rewrites code to use what PHP 8.4 added. Behind each feature is a decision that a project can also turn on without the set, for example with `upgrading.functions.roundingMode: required`.

| feature | before | after | fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| `array_any()`, `array_all()`, `array_find()`, `array_find_key()`, `upgrading.functions.arraySearchFunctions` | a `foreach` that returns the first item that matches | `array_find($items, fn($item) => $item->sku === $sku)` | fixed; risky where the loop may go through an object |
| the same functions instead of `array_filter()` | `array_key_first(array_filter($a, fn($v) => $v === 0))` | `array_find_key($a, fn($v) => $v === 0)` | fixed; risky where the callback may do more than compare |
| `RoundingMode`, `upgrading.functions.roundingMode` | `round($x, 2, PHP_ROUND_HALF_UP)` | `round($x, 2, RoundingMode::HalfAwayFromZero)` | fixed |
| `new` without parentheses, `classes.instantiationWrapping` | `(new Foo())->bar()` | `new Foo()->bar()` | fixed; the standards `perCs` and `nette` require it too |
| `#[\Deprecated]`, `upgrading.phpdoc.deprecated` | `/** @deprecated 2.1 use format() */` | `#[\Deprecated('use format()', since: '2.1')]` | risky: PHP then reports every use of the deprecated code |

.[note]
A rewrite for PHP 8.4 does not get ahead of the project. As long as `require.php` in `composer.json` allows PHP 8.3, no `array_find()` appears in the code, and `dresscode config` marks the decision with `no effect: php`.


What PHP 8.4 Deprecated
=======================

The set `deprecations` fixes what has a direct replacement and reports the rest, with the version that deprecated it. Like the rewrites to new features, it follows the lower bound in `composer.json`: the deprecations of PHP 8.4 are reported once the project requires at least PHP 8.4.

| deprecated | DressCode |
|---|---|
| a parameter made nullable by its default alone, `int $x = null` | fixed: `?int $x = null` |
| the default `escape` of `fputcsv()`, `fgetcsv()` and `str_getcsv()` | fixed: the argument `escape` is written out with the backslash it had by default |
| `E_STRICT` | reported |
| `E_USER_ERROR` passed to `trigger_error()` | reported: throw an exception instead |
| `_` as the name of a class | reported |
| `lcg_value()`, `mysqli_ping()`, `mysqli_kill()`, `mysqli_refresh()`, `xml_set_object()`, `intlcal_set()`, `intlgregcal_create_instance()` | reported |
| `DOM_PHP_ERR`, `SOAP_FUNCTIONS_ALL`, `SUNFUNCS_RET_*`, `MYSQLI_STORE_RESULT_COPY_DATA` | reported |

Each finding names the PHP version that deprecated it:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Bootstrap.php:11:34: error: Constant `E_STRICT` is deprecated: since PHP 8.4. [upgrading.php.deprecatedApi]
src/Config.php:17:47: error: Passing `E_USER_ERROR` to `trigger_error()` is deprecated since PHP 8.4 in favor of throwing an exception. [upgrading.php.deprecatedArgument]
\--


What Is Left to You
===================

DressCode does not find everything PHP 8.4 deprecated. It leaves alone the CSV methods of `SplFileObject`, because the code does not say which object a call is made on. It does not report the constants `CURLOPT_BINARYTRANSFER`, `MYSQLI_REFRESH_*` and `SID`, a `DatePeriod` created from a string, raising zero to a negative power, or the session settings such as `session.sid_length`. Check these by hand against the [UPGRADING file of PHP 8.4 |https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/php-8.4.0/UPGRADING], which lists every deprecation of the version.


Risky Fixes
===========

A rewrite that may change what the code does waits until you list its decision in `fixRisky`. For PHP 8.4, this is mainly `#[\Deprecated]`. The annotation `@deprecated` only informs the reader, while the attribute makes PHP report a deprecation at every use. DressCode moves the version from the annotation to `since` and the rest of the text to the message. In PHP 8.4, the attribute goes on a function, a method, a class constant and an enum case; a trait and a constant declared with `const` accept it only since PHP 8.5.

```neon
use:
	- perCs
	- modernizations

fixRisky:
	- upgrading.phpdoc.deprecated

targets:
	php: '8.4'
```

```php .[before]
class Invoice
{
    /** @deprecated 2.1 use format() */
    public function toString(): string
    {
        return $this->format();
    }
}
```

```php .[after]
class Invoice
{
    #[\Deprecated('use format()', since: '2.1')]
    public function toString(): string
    {
        return $this->format();
    }
}
```

The new array functions take only an array, and they call the callback only until they know the answer. So the rewrite waits for a `foreach` over an `iterable` or over a value of unknown type, and for `array_filter()` with a callback that calls a method, because such a callback might count its calls or throw an exception on a later item. [Types from PHPStan |phpstan-types] tell DressCode which loops go through an array; you decide the rest one by one with `dresscode fix --ask-risky`.

.[warning]
Inside a namespace, `round()` or `fputcsv()` without a leading backslash may mean a function of that namespace. DressCode rewrites such calls only when the configuration says `nameResolution: certain`; until then, they wait as risky, see [Functions and Constants in Namespaces |php-functions-in-namespaces].


Property Hooks
==============

PHP 8.4 also added property hooks, code that runs when a property is read or written. They are new syntax, not a shorter form of an old one, so no decision rewrites your code to them. But DressCode reads them like any other code. Its parser [PhpSyntax |phpsyntax:] builds a syntax tree from the grammar of PHP, so the body of a property hook is a body like any other, and both the rewrites and the rules of your standard reach inside it:

```neon
use:
	- perCs
	- modernizations

targets:
	php: '8.4'
```

```php .[before]
final class User
{
    public string $email {
        set(string $value) {
            if (strpos($value, '@') === false) { throw new ValueError('Invalid e-mail'); }
            $this->email = strtolower($value);
        }
    }
}
```

```php .[after]
final class User
{
    public string $email {
        set(string $value) {
            if (!str_contains($value, '@')) {
                throw new ValueError('Invalid e-mail');
            }
            $this->email = strtolower($value);
        }
    }
}
```

Tools whose rules work directly with a flat array of tokens have a harder job with new syntax, and it is not the fault of their authors: every rule has to learn it on its own. Almost two years after the release of PHP 8.4, the [issue of PHP_CodeSniffer |https://github.com/PHPCSStandards/PHP_CodeSniffer/issues/734] that tracks the new syntax still lists support for the property hook syntax in its tokenizer as work to be done.


Step by Step
============

DressCode itself needs PHP 8.4, so for a project that still runs on PHP 8.3, install it [globally |upgrade#Where DressCode Runs], not as a development dependency. The [upgrade plan |upgrade-plan] needs a configuration, `dresscode.neon`; if the project does not have one yet, `dresscode init` measures it from the code.

1. Commit or stash your work in progress, so that the diff holds only what DressCode changed.
2. Find out how many places will change: `dresscode upgrade estimate php 8.4`.
3. Plan the upgrade and carry it out: `dresscode upgrade plan php 8.4 --save`, then `dresscode upgrade run`. DressCode asks before it raises `require.php` to `^8.4`; if you raise it yourself, just run `upgrade run` afterwards.
4. Go through what DressCode left for you, including the risky fixes that wait for types, for `nameResolution` or for your decision, and repeat `upgrade run` until it ends with `UPGRADED`.
5. Commit the upgrade separately from other changes.

When you raise `require.php` yourself, you do not need the plan: if the configuration has the sets `modernizations` and `deprecations`, run `dresscode fix`, otherwise [`dresscode upgrade today --modernize` |upgrade#When You Have Already Raised the Versions]. [The Upgrade Plan Step by Step |upgrade-plan] covers the whole procedure of the plan, with tests, commits and manual tasks.


